From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 12:36:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] msspellcheckingfacility: Add the Windows spell checking API,
 with the host's Hunspell dictionaries.

Word creates the spell checker factory at every start, to register
Office's custom dictionary for every language; Wine had no such class
(the only error Word logged at start).  Measured on Windows 11 build
29671 with tools/spellprobe:

- The factory lists the languages of the host's Hunspell dictionaries,
  as tags (en_US is en-US), in order; IsSupported and CreateSpellChecker
  take a tag in any case and nothing else (no "en" for "en-US"), an
  empty one being E_INVALIDARG; a checker gives back its tag as it was
  asked for.  Its id is MsSpell, its name "Microsoft Windows Spell
  Checker" in the user's language, and it has no options.
- Checking: words in capitals, with digits, addresses and mail
  addresses are not checked; apostrophes and hyphens join a word, and
  in a compound only the wrong parts are errors; a word that repeats
  the one before, with only spaces between them and in any case, is to
  be deleted; a word with a correction is to be replaced.  A
  comprehensive check gives the replacement when there is only one
  suggestion.  Errors with no replacement give an empty one.
- The user's words are Windows': added words in
  %APPDATA%\Microsoft\Spelling\neutral\default.dic for every language,
  removed ones in <language>\default.exc, corrections in
  <language>\default.acl as "word|correction", UTF-16 with a byte order
  mark.  They are read again when they change, and changed under a
  mutex the processes share.  Ignored words are the checker's own.
  Adding tells every checker once, removing and correcting twice, as
  Windows does.
- A user dictionary can be registered for a language, any tag, or for
  every language with an empty one, as Office does; those for a
  language are kept as Windows keeps them, in
  HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Spelling\Dictionaries.

Hunspell is loaded at run time; without it, or without dictionaries,
no language is supported.  Its suggestions are not Windows' own:
"wrold" has two, so a comprehensive check offers to choose where
Windows replaces it (todo_wine).  The interfaces are in
include/spellcheck.idl, their IIDs in libuuid.  The tests have no
failures on Windows (341) or under Wine; they put the user's word
lists back as they were.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

